{{also|Strawberry}}
==English==
[[Image:Strawberries in white bowl.jpg|thumb|Strawberries in a bowl]]

===Etymology===
From {{inh|en|enm|strawbery}}, {{m|enm|strauberi}}, from {{inh|en|ang|strēawberġe}}, corresponding to {{compound|en|straw|berry}}. The word for straw was derived from a verbal participle and thus meant "(that which is) strewn", hence the applicability to berries growing as if they have been “strewn” about the ground.

===Pronunciation===
* {{a|UK}} {{IPA|en|/ˈstɹɔːb(ə)ɹi/}}
* {{a|US}} {{IPA|en|/ˈstɹɔˌbɛɹi/}}
* {{a|cot-caught}} {{IPA|en|/ˈstɹɑˌbɛɹi/}}
* {{audio|en|En-us-strawberry.ogg|Audio (US, California)}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun|~}}

# The sweet, usually [[red]], edible [[fruit]] of certain plants of the genus ''[[Fragaria]]''.
#: {{ux|en|They went to pick '''strawberries''' today.}}
# Any plant of the genus ''Fragaria'' (that bears such fruit).
#: {{ux|en|She has the best '''strawberry''' patch I've ever seen.}}
#* {{quote-book|1=en|year=1886|translator=H.L. Br&aelig;kstad|author=Peter Christen Asbj&oslash;rnsen|authorlink=Peter Christen Asbj&oslash;rnsen|title=Folk and Fairy Tales|page=170|passage=He told his father, and said it would be just suitable work for him to run about fields and woods amongst the '''strawberry''' hills after a flock of hares, and now and then lie down and take a nap on some sunny hill.}}
# A dark [[pinkish]] [[red]] [[color|colour]], like that of the fruit; [[strawberry red]].
#: {{color panel|B64140}}
# {{lb|en|rare}} Something resembling a strawberry, especially a reddish bruise, [[birthmark]], or infantile [[hemangioma]] ([[naevus]]).
# {{lb|en|US|slang}} A [[prostitute]] who exchanges sexual services for [[crack cocaine]].
#* '''1992''', Kathleen Boyle, ''Homeless crack cocaine abusers'' (page 40)
#*: {{...}} infamous in Los Angeles through media reports: the crack houses and "'''strawberries'''" (women who exchange sex for crack) {{...}}
#* '''1997''', Peter Collier, David Horowitz, ''The Race Card'' (page 91)
#*: The desperate addiction associated with the drug has made "'''strawberries'''" — prostitutes who work for crack — fixtures of the {{...}}

